[
Usage: [ EXPRESSION ]

Evaluate EXPRESSION and exit 0 if true, 1 if false.

The bracket form of `test`: the final argument must be a literal ].
This is what `if [ ... ]` in a shell script actually runs.

	-e FILE		FILE exists
	-f FILE		FILE is a regular file
	-d FILE		FILE is a directory
	-r FILE		FILE is readable
	-w FILE		FILE is writable
	-x FILE		FILE is executable
	-s FILE		Exists and isn't empty
	-L FILE		FILE is a symbolic link
	-z STR		STR is empty
	-n STR		STR is not empty
	N1 -eq N2	Numbers are equal
	N1 -gt N2	First number is bigger
	N1 -lt N2	First number is smaller
	N1 -ne,-le,-ge N2	not equal, less/equal, greater/equal
	STR1 = STR2	strings are equal
	STR1 != STR2	strings differ
	! EXPR		negate
	EXPR -a EXPR	and;  EXPR -o EXPR	or

Quote your variables: [ -n $x ] breaks when x is empty or has spaces,
[ -n "$x" ] does not.

See also: test
